---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ToolIcon from '../components/ToolIcon.astro';
import { tools } from '../lib/tools';
import { SITE_NAME } from '../lib/site';
import { websiteSchema, faqSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const features = [
  {
    title: 'Exact quantities',
    text: 'Bags, cubic yards, or tons — precise numbers you can hand straight to the supplier, with waste already built in.',
    icon: 'target',
  },
  {
    title: 'Math you can audit',
    text: 'Every tool shows its formula and assumptions, so you can verify the figure instead of trusting a black box.',
    icon: 'formula',
  },
  {
    title: 'Free & private',
    text: 'No accounts, no sign-up. Calculations run instantly in your browser and your inputs never leave your device.',
    icon: 'shield',
  },
];

const slabSizes = ['10x10', '10x12', '10x20', '12x12', '12x16', '12x24', '16x20', '20x20', '20x30', '24x24', '24x30', '30x30'];
const gravelLengths = [50, 60, 80, 100, 150, 200, 300, 400];

const homeFaqs = [
  {
    question: 'How many bags of concrete do I need for a 10x10 slab?',
    answer:
      'A 10x10 slab at 4 inches thick is 1.23 cubic yards, which takes 56 bags of 80 lb mix without waste and about 62 with 10%. The concrete bags calculator gives the count for any slab size, and the 10x10 slab guide breaks the whole pour down step by step.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How many bags of concrete in a yard?',
    answer:
      'Forty-five bags of 80 lb mix, 60 of 60 lb, 72 of 50 lb or 90 of 40 lb — a cubic yard is 27 cubic feet and every bag prints its yield. The bags-per-yard page has the full table with 2026 prices per yard.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much gravel do I need for a driveway?',
    answer:
      'Figure 4 inches deep for cars and 6 inches total if trucks use it, at 1.4 tons per cubic yard. A 50 ft driveway at 10 ft wide takes about 8.6 tons; the gravel driveway calculator converts your exact length, width and depth into yards and tons.',
  },
  {
    question: 'When is a bag job too big for bags?',
    answer:
      'Around 1.5 cubic yards. Below that, bags beat the short-load delivery fee; above 2 yards, delivered ready-mix wins on price, labor and set time. Every calculator on this site shows the crossover for your volume.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  websiteSchema(Astro.site ?? new URL(Astro.url.origin)),
  faqSchema(homeFaqs),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Concrete & Gravel Calculators — Bags, Slabs, Footings"
  description="Free concrete and gravel calculators: bags, cubic yards, slabs, footings, fence posts, driveways, frost depth. Exact quantities, math shown, no sign-up."
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <span class="hero-badge"><span class="dot"></span>Free · Instant · No sign-up</span>
    <h1>Concrete &amp; Gravel Calculators — <span class="accent">order the right amount, every time.</span></h1>
    <p class="lead">Before you buy, know exactly how much material to get. Each tool does one job,
      runs instantly in your browser, and shows its math — so you stop over-ordering or running short.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="btn" href="#calculators">Browse calculators</a>
      <a class="btn-ghost" href="#guides">Popular guides</a>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>{tools.length}</b><span>Pro-grade tools</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>80+</b><span>Guides & pages</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>100%</b><span>Free, forever</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>&lt;1s</b><span>Instant results</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== AdSense: top of page (replace comment block with your <ins> tag) =====
  <div class="ad"><div class="box">[AdSense — top banner]</div></div>
  -->

  <div class="section-head" id="calculators">
    <span class="eyebrow">Our calculators</span>
    <h2>Pick a tool, get your number</h2>
    <p>Purpose-built for the most common DIY concrete and gravel jobs around the house.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tools">
    {tools.map((t) => (
      <a class="tool" href={`/${t.slug}/`}>
        <span class="tool-icon"><ToolIcon slug={t.slug} /></span>
        <b>{t.title}</b>
        <span>{t.blurb}</span>
        <span class="go">
          Open tool
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M5 12h14" />
            <path d="m12 5 7 7-7 7" />
          </svg>
        </span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>

  <!-- ===== AdSense: bottom of hub =====
  <div class="ad"><div class="box">[AdSense — bottom banner]</div></div>
  -->

  <div class="section-head" id="guides">
    <span class="eyebrow">Start here</span>
    <h2>Concrete: two questions cover most pours</h2>
    <p>Almost every bagged-concrete problem is one of these two questions. Both pages carry the full
      tables, the formulas and the bag-size trade-offs.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="card">
    <h3>Question 1 — count for your pour</h3>
    <p>
      You have a slab, pad or set of post holes drawn out, and you need the number of bags to buy.
      That is the <a href="/concrete-bags-calculator/">how many bags of concrete do I need</a>
      calculator: enter length, width and thickness, pick 80, 60, 50 or 40 lb bags, and it returns
      the exact count rounded up with a waste margin already included — plus the cubic-yard figure
      for the day the truck becomes the better option. For a 10x10 slab at 4 inches the answer is
      56 bags of 80 lb mix; the calculator scales that to any footprint in one step.
    </p>
    <h3>Question 2 — the yard conversion</h3>
    <p>
      Quotes, delivery minimums and pallet pricing all speak in cubic yards, while the home center
      sells bags. The bridge between the two is
      <a href="/how-many-bags-of-concrete-in-a-yard/">how many bags of concrete in a yard</a>:
      45 bags of 80 lb mix, 60 of 60 lb, 72 of 50 lb or 90 of 40 lb, because a yard is 27 cubic feet
      and each bag yields a fixed slice of it. That page also prices a full yard in bags at 2026
      home-center rates — $315 to $405 — and marks the volume where delivered ready-mix takes over.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything else hangs off those two. The <a href="/concrete-calculator/">concrete calculator</a>
      gives you volume in cubic feet and yards first; the
      <a href="/concrete-slab-cost-calculator/">slab cost calculator</a> turns volume into a material
      budget; the <a href="/concrete-bag-coverage-calculator/">bag coverage calculator</a> works
      backwards from square footage to bag count at a fixed thickness; and the
      <a href="/concrete-fence-post-calculator/">fence post calculator</a> subtracts the post volume
      from each hole so a 60-post fence does not over-order by a dozen bags.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Slab guides by size, bag counts worked out</h2>
    <p>
      Twelve sizes cover nearly every pour people ask about, from a 10x10 shed floor to a 30x30
      garage pad. Each guide has the bag table for 80/60/40 lb mix, a thickness chart from 4 to 6
      inches, and the bags-versus-truck verdict for that footprint:
    </p>
    <div class="related">
      {slabSizes.map((s) => (
        <a href={`/how-many-bags-of-concrete-for-a-${s}-slab/`}>{s} slab</a>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p>
      Sizes are clickable because the math scales cleanly: square footage drives cubic yards, cubic
      yards drive bag count, and only the mixing time and the short-load fee change the verdict.
      A 12x12 pad at 4 inches is 1.33 cubic yards — still a bag job; a 24x30 at 5 inches is over 11
      cubic yards — call the plant.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Gravel: driveways, roads and paths</h2>
    <p>
      Gravel is sold by the ton and moved by the truckload, so the calculators here convert your
      length, width and depth into cubic yards <em>and</em> tons — at 1.4 tons per cubic yard for
      crushed stone — then price the stone at 2026 delivered rates of $30–$60 per ton. The main
      <a href="/gravel-driveway-calculator/">gravel driveway calculator</a> handles the standard
      pour-in-place drive; the <a href="/gravel-driveway-cost-calculator/">cost calculator</a> adds
      the material budget, the <a href="/gravel-driveway-depth-calculator/">depth calculator</a>
      splits base and surface layers for cars versus trucks, the
      <a href="/gravel-road-calculator/">gravel road calculator</a> sizes longer private roads, and
      the <a href="/bulk-bag-vs-bags-calculator/">bulk-bag-vs-bags comparison</a> settles whether
      super sacks or 0.5 cu ft bags are cheaper for small jobs. The
      <a href="/gravel-driveway-estimate/">driveway estimate page</a> rolls material, delivery and
      total cost into one number.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the eight driveway lengths people actually build — 50 to 400 feet — there is a worked
      example for each, with the two-layer 4-inch-base-plus-2-inch-surface build and truckload
      counts:
    </p>
    <div class="related">
      {gravelLengths.map((l) => (
        <a href={`/how-much-gravel-for-a-${l}-ft-driveway/`}>{l} ft driveway</a>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p>
      Companion materials are covered too: the <a href="/paver-sand-calculator/">paver sand
      calculator</a> for bedding layers and the <a href="/mulch-calculator/">mulch calculator</a>
      for beds — both convert square feet into the yards or bags you actually order.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Frost depth decides how deep your footings go</h2>
    <p>
      Any footing — post holes, deck piers, slab edges — has to reach below the frost line, or
      freeze-thaw heave will lift it a little every winter until doors stick and posts lean. Code
      depth varies from 12 inches in the Deep South to 60 inches in the northern states, and it is
      set state by state (and sometimes county by county). The
      <a href="/frost-depth-by-state/">frost depth by state</a> hub lists the code footing depth,
      the practical dig depth and the bags of concrete per post hole for all 50 states, with major
      cities broken out — so the fence-post math and the footer math both start from the right
      number for where you live.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="section-head" id="why">
    <span class="eyebrow">Why it matters</span>
    <h2>Why calculate before you buy?</h2>
    <p>Concrete and gravel are sold by the bag or the cubic yard, and most people guess. A slab an
      inch too thick or a driveway a foot too short wastes real money.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="features">
    {features.map((f) => (
      <div class="feature">
        <span class="tool-icon">
          {f.icon === 'target' && (
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
              <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" />
              <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="6" />
              <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="2" />
            </svg>
          )}
          {f.icon === 'formula' && (
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
              <rect width="16" height="20" x="4" y="2" rx="2" />
              <line x1="8" x2="16" y1="6" y2="6" />
              <path d="m8 12 3 3-3 3" />
              <path d="M13 18h4" />
            </svg>
          )}
          {f.icon === 'shield' && (
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M20 13c0 5-3.5 7.5-7.66 8.95a1 1 0 0 1-.67-.01C7.5 20.5 4 18 4 13V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1c2 0 4.5-1.2 6.24-2.72a1.17 1.17 0 0 1 1.52 0C14.51 3.81 17 5 19 5a1 1 0 0 1 1 1z" />
              <path d="m9 12 2 2 4-4" />
            </svg>
          )}
        </span>
        <h3>{f.title}</h3>
        <p>{f.text}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>The math behind every calculator on this site</h2>
    <p>
      None of it is hidden, and none of it is complicated. Concrete volume is a box:
      <code>length × width × (thickness ÷ 12) = cubic feet</code>, divided by 27 for cubic yards.
      Bag counts are the same box divided by yield:
      <code>bags = cubic feet ÷ yield per bag</code> — an 80 lb bag yields 0.60 cubic feet, so a
      full 27-cubic-foot yard is 27 ÷ 0.60 = 45 bags, a 60 lb bag yields 0.45 for 60 bags per yard,
      and so on. Gravel follows the same volume math with a density step: cubic yards × 1.4 = tons
      of crushed stone.
    </p>
    <p>
      The assumptions differ per tool and each page states them: 10% waste on formed slabs because
      formwork bows and subgrade settles, 4 inches minimum for a car driveway and 6 inches total for
      trucks, and the 1.5-cubic-yard crossover where bags stop beating the short-load fee. If a
      number here disagrees with your supplier's quote, check the assumptions section on that
      calculator's page — every input is labeled and nothing is rounded silently.
    </p>
    <p>
      A note on the bag math people get wrong most often: yields are fixed by weight, not by brand.
      Whatever the logo on the bag, an 80 lb pre-blended mix yields about 0.60 cubic feet — premium
      fast-set and high-strength blends yield slightly less, closer to 0.55, which moves a full yard
      from 45 bags toward 50. Check the printed yield when you switch products.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      {homeFaqs.map((f) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{f.question}</summary>
          <p>{f.answer}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
